entire trail list — List trails in a project
List trails in one explicitly selected --project, newest update first, without requiring a checkout. --project is required even inside a clone; --repo only filters within that project. Requests go directly to the project's assigned cell. --status filters on the server before pagination. Use --page-token with the same project and filters to continue. Omitting --status includes all lifecycle states.

--mine instead lists the trails you authored or are assigned, across every project and region, latest activity first. It returns one page of up to --limit trails and takes no --project, --repo, or --page-token; a region that cannot be reached is reported as a warning.

When to use this:
Trail commands talk to entire.io and read the saved login from the OS
keyring (the Keychain on macOS), so they need network and keyring access.
If you run commands in a sandbox, request unsandboxed execution for
`entire trail` up front. An error saying the Keychain is not accessible or
the network is not allowed means the sandbox blocked it, not that the user
is logged out: ask to re-run it unsandboxed, and do not run `entire login`.

A project trail spans repositories; each change is its branch in one
repository. Project trails and changes are numbered separately: never
pass <P> where a change is expected, or <n> where a project trail is.
Pass <repo>/<n> to checkout, resume, finding, watch, approvals or
approve; given to show, it shows the parent project trail.
Users paste Entire trail URLs. Do not pass a URL to any command; map
the path after the host to a selector:
  <forge>/<project>/-/trails/<P>/changes/<repo>/<n>  change <repo>/<n>
  <forge>/<project>/<repo>/-/trails/<P>/changes/<n>  change <repo>/<n>
  <forge>/<project>/<repo>/trails/<n>                change <repo>/<n>
  <forge>/<project>/-/trails/<P>                     project trail <P>
Add --project <forge>/<project> unless <project> is origin's owner.
To link a trail or change, use the url `entire trail show` prints.

Scope: one project; --project is required, even inside a clone. --repo only filters within that project. --mine instead lists your trails across every project.

Flags:
      --json[=true|false]   Output raw JSON instead of a table
  -n, --limit int           Page size (1-100) (default 50)
      --mine[=true|false]   List trails you authored or are assigned, across every project and region
      --page-token string   Continue from nextPageToken
      --status string       Filter by status before pagination (open, closed)

Inherited flags:
      --context name     Act as this saved login name for this command only — including the git and agent processes it spawns — instead of the active context (entire auth contexts lists them)
      --project string   Project as gh/<owner> or et/<project>; required for list, otherwise defaults to the repository's namespace
      --repo string      Target repository as forge/owner/repo (e.g. gh/acme/app) or a clone URL; for list, only filters the required --project
